1
00:00:01,478 --> 00:00:09,965
Welcome to Ukraine's regions, part 2 of 2. First, I clear the chosen names for
a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10,623 --> 00:00:22,349
We work through 15 of the Ukraine regions the map names, once each: where it
sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:23,055 --> 00:00:24,889
Let's bring Sumy into view.

4
00:00:35,657 --> 00:00:47,255
Sumy is in the north-east of Ukraine, with Chernihiv to the west, Poltava to
the south-west and Kharkiv to the south, and its largest city is the town of
Sumy.

5
00:00:47,910 --> 00:00:58,614
It was founded in 1655 by Cossacks moving east away from Polish rule, and its
name comes from sumka, a bag or pouch.

6
00:00:59,271 --> 00:01:05,413
Remember the three hunting bags on the arms of a town that grew rich on sugar.

7
00:01:06,134 --> 00:01:08,607
Let's bring Poltava into view.

8
00:01:14,416 --> 00:01:29,904
Poltava is in the centre of Ukraine, with Sumy to the north, Kharkiv to the
east, Kyiv Oblast to the west and Dnipropetrovsk to the south-east, and
Poltava as its largest city.

9
00:01:30,569 --> 00:01:37,907
Peter the Great defeated the Swedes here in 1709, and that battle ended
Sweden’s time as a great power.

10
00:01:38,561 --> 00:01:46,282
Remember the central region where the writer Gogol was born, and the local
dialect that became the base of literary Ukrainian.

11
00:01:47,075 --> 00:01:48,991
Let's bring Kharkiv into view.

12
00:01:59,151 --> 00:02:13,551
Kharkiv is in the east of Ukraine, with Sumy to the north and Poltava to the
north-west, and its border with Russia runs along the eastern side for three
hundred kilometres, with Kharkiv as its largest city.

13
00:02:14,214 --> 00:02:24,663
It was the capital of Soviet Ukraine until 1934, which is why it has the
constructivist Derzhprom, one of the first skyscrapers in Europe.

14
00:02:25,330 --> 00:02:32,702
Remember Freedom Square, one of the largest in Europe, and the river Kharkiv
that lent the city its name.

15
00:02:33,537 --> 00:02:35,789
Let's bring Cherkasy into view.

16
00:02:45,713 --> 00:03:00,551
Cherkasy is in the centre of Ukraine, with Kyiv Oblast to the north, Poltava
to the north-east, Vinnytsya to the west and Kirovohrad to the south, and
Cherkasy as its largest city.

17
00:03:01,207 --> 00:03:10,890
The Dnipro widens here into the Kremenchuk Reservoir, about a hundred and
fifty kilometres long, the largest body of water in the country.

18
00:03:11,552 --> 00:03:18,332
Remember the reservoir spreading out below the river, with the poet Shevchenko
buried on a hill above it.

19
00:03:19,117 --> 00:03:21,242
Let's bring Vinnytsya into view.

20
00:03:31,179 --> 00:03:44,832
Vinnytsya is in the centre of Ukraine, with Zhytomyr to the north-west, Kyiv
Oblast to the north, Kirovohrad to the east and Odesa to the south, and the
Southern Buh runs through its largest city.

21
00:03:45,498 --> 00:03:52,870
Its fountain on the Southern Buh is the largest floating fountain in Europe,
shooting water seventy metres into the air.

22
00:03:53,522 --> 00:04:02,601
Remember the central region whose name probably comes from vino, an old Slavic
word for a dowry paid in land.

23
00:04:03,455 --> 00:04:05,498
Let's bring Luhansk into view.

24
00:04:18,178 --> 00:04:29,695
Luhansk is in the far east of Ukraine, with Kharkiv to the north-west and
Donetsk to the west, and its largest city sits on the river Luhan near the
Russian border.

25
00:04:30,359 --> 00:04:38,625
It was founded in 1795 around a British-built cannon foundry, made to arm the
fleet on the Black Sea.

26
00:04:39,290 --> 00:04:47,986
Remember the eastern steppe, some of the last unploughed grassland in Europe,
protected in reserves since 1968.

27
00:04:48,706 --> 00:04:51,516
Let's bring Kirovohrad into view.

28
00:04:57,002 --> 00:05:10,052
Kirovohrad is in the centre of Ukraine, with Cherkasy to the north, Poltava to
the north-east, Dnipropetrovsk to the east and Odesa and Mykolayiv to the
south, and Kirovohrad as its largest city.

29
00:05:10,718 --> 00:05:20,482
The map still carries the Soviet name of Sergei Kirov, and since 2016 the city
itself has been called Kropyvnytskyi, after a playwright.

30
00:05:21,141 --> 00:05:27,155
Remember the region holding the geographical centre of the country, marked by
a monument in a field.

31
00:05:27,949 --> 00:05:31,269
Let's bring Dnipropetrovsk into view.

32
00:05:40,350 --> 00:05:56,226
Dnipropetrovsk is in the east of Ukraine, on the middle Dnipro, with Poltava
to the north, Kharkiv to the north-east and Zaporizhzhya and Kherson to the
south, and its largest city is now simply called Dnipro.

33
00:05:56,881 --> 00:06:07,028
The city was founded as Katerynoslav, Catherine’s glory, by Potemkin for
Catherine the Great, and it was closed to foreigners until 1987.

34
00:06:07,681 --> 00:06:14,415
Remember the rocket works on the middle river, and the city that dropped the
second half of its name in 2016.

35
00:06:15,176 --> 00:06:17,475
Let's bring Donetsk into view.

36
00:06:26,814 --> 00:06:38,795
Donetsk is in the east of Ukraine, with Kharkiv to the north, Luhansk to the
east and Zaporizhzhya and Dnipropetrovsk to the west, and Donetsk as its
largest city, on the Siverskyi Donets river.

37
00:06:39,457 --> 00:06:51,439
The city was founded in 1869 by a Welsh ironmaster, John Hughes, and was
called Yuzivka for fifty years, a Russian rendering of his name.

38
00:06:52,088 --> 00:06:59,971
Remember the mine workings honeycombing the ground, and the spoil heaps that
form the best-known skyline outside the city.

39
00:07:00,800 --> 00:07:03,865
Let's bring Mykolayiv into view.

40
00:07:13,209 --> 00:07:26,003
Mykolayiv is on the Black Sea coast in the south of Ukraine, on the estuary of
the Southern Bug, with Odesa to the west and Kherson to the east, and
Mykolayiv as its largest city.

41
00:07:26,669 --> 00:07:33,751
Most of the Soviet Union’s aircraft carriers were built in this city, on a
river a hundred kilometres from the sea.

42
00:07:34,404 --> 00:07:42,763
Remember the southern shipyard city with the oldest working observatory in the
region, recording since 1821.

43
00:07:43,520 --> 00:07:46,794
Let's bring Zaporizhzhya into view.

44
00:07:55,028 --> 00:08:08,635
Zaporizhzhya is in the south-east of Ukraine, with Donetsk to the north-east,
Dnipropetrovsk to the north-west and the Black Sea coast along its southern
edge, and Zaporizhzhya as its largest city.

45
00:08:09,300 --> 00:08:19,064
Its name means beyond the rapids, and those Dnipro cataracts were the site of
the first Cossack Sich in the 1550s.

46
00:08:19,722 --> 00:08:27,013
Remember the rapids drowned by the 1932 dam, with the Sich island of
Khortytsia still standing in the reservoir.

47
00:08:27,703 --> 00:08:30,896
Let's bring Odesa into view.

48
00:08:34,949 --> 00:08:46,501
Odesa is on the Black Sea coast in the south of Ukraine, with Mykolayiv to the
east and Kirovohrad and Vinnytsya inland to the north, and Odesa as its
largest city.

49
00:08:47,167 --> 00:08:55,267
The name was given in 1795 after Odessos, a Greek colony that was thought to
have stood nearby but never did.

50
00:08:55,918 --> 00:09:05,856
Remember the free port that filled with Greeks, Italians, Jews and Frenchmen,
and the long Potemkin Steps down to the harbour.

51
00:09:06,536 --> 00:09:08,579
Let's bring Kherson into view.

52
00:09:13,623 --> 00:09:27,056
Kherson is on the Black Sea coast in the south of Ukraine, with Mykolayiv to
the west, Crimea across the strait to the south and Dnipropetrovsk to the
north, and Kherson as its largest city.

53
00:09:27,711 --> 00:09:35,211
The first shipyard on the Black Sea was built here, before Mykolayiv and
before Sevastopol.

54
00:09:35,868 --> 00:09:44,355
Remember the southern region where the Dnipro fans into a delta of two
thousand islands and channels before it reaches the sea.

55
00:09:45,050 --> 00:09:47,813
Let's bring Crimea into view.

56
00:09:51,976 --> 00:10:06,988
Crimea is the peninsula in the south-east of Ukraine, across the strait from
Kherson, with Sevastopol shown separately on this map at its south-west
corner, and Simferopol as its largest city.

57
00:10:07,641 --> 00:10:20,435
Greeks, Scythians, Goths, Genoese, Tatars, Ottomans and Russians have all held
the peninsula over the centuries, and the Tatars were deported wholesale in
1944.

58
00:10:21,095 --> 00:10:28,467
Remember the subtropical southern coast sheltered by the mountains, where the
tsars and then the Politburo holidayed.

59
00:10:29,148 --> 00:10:31,749
Let's bring Sevastopol into view.

60
00:10:36,052 --> 00:10:45,990
Sevastopol is the small harbour city on the southern coast of Ukraine, at the
foot of the Crimean peninsula, and the map shows it separately from Crimea.

61
00:10:46,641 --> 00:10:55,210
The name was coined in 1783 from the Greek for venerable city, one of
Catherine the Great’s classical inventions.

62
00:10:55,861 --> 00:11:04,255
Remember the harbour besieged for eleven months in the Crimean War, with the
Greek ruins of Chersonesus inside the modern city.

63
00:11:09,980 --> 00:11:31,832
15 regions of Ukraine, one for each target of the map: Sumy, Poltava, Kharkiv,
Cherkasy, Vinnytsya, Luhansk, Kirovohrad, Dnipropetrovsk, Donetsk, Mykolayiv,
Zaporizhzhya, Odesa, Kherson, Crimea and Sevastopol.

64
00:11:32,486 --> 00:11:43,829
Remember the shape of Ukraine from the inside: the regions nest around each
other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
